  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 AM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Areas of early morning fog will improve by mid \nmorning. Isolated shower or storm possible far SW through late \nafternoon, and over the NE tonight.\n\nAreas of fog continue to form early this morning, mostly through the \ncentral Piedmont into the N Sandhills, some of it locally dense. \nGiven the nearly calm air and high moisture content in the low \nlevels early this morning (via obs and 00z regional 925 mb \nanalysis), we should continue to see low clouds and areas of fog \nthrough much of the morning, as supported by GLAMP guidance and most \nHREF/REFS members. With light winds expected to hold today from the \nsurface through the first few thousand ft AGL today, diurnal mixing \nwill be hampered, leading to a slow lifting and breakup of low \nclouds and delayed heating. Will continue to lean just under NBM and \nmost statistical guidance today, with highs generally in the low-mid \n80s. \n\nThe surface front remains just S and SW of the CWA according to the \nlatest analysis, from W NC across NE SC, with a large cluster of \nconvection currently just S of the CLT area, just SW of our CWA. As \nthis front wavers today with additional convection expected to ride \nalong the front/CAPE gradient, isold to sctd showers and storms may \nedge into our far W and SW CWA, from mid morning through late \nafternoon, as a subtle shortwave trough evident on WV imagery tracks \nfrom the OH Valley SE through W and S NC, into the mean longwave \ntrough base. Another shortwave is expected to track from MI this \nmorning SE into the Mid Atlantic region tonight, which is expected \nto bring another chance of isold-sctd showers/storms across NE \nsections from late evening through the overnight hours. The degree \nof destabilization with both potential rounds of convection is \nhighly uncertain given the abundant low level moisture and \nlimitations in surface heating, along with the lower PW as compared \nto past days. But our mid level flow will become stronger and more \ncyclonic late today through tonight, supporting more organized \nconvection, so an isolated strong storm can't be ruled out. But \noverall coverage in our area is expected to be limited. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A cold front passage late Tuesday will bring the \nnext chance for showers/tstms with isolated severe storms possible.\n\nA short wave trough currently crossing the Pacific NW will spend the \nnext two days moving around the northern periphery of the ridge \nthat's centered over the Plains and Rockies.  By late Tuesday it \nwill drop down the east side of the ridge and into the back side of \nthe l/w trough that's just off the East Coast.  In doing so, it will \nmove across the Mid Atlantic region, deepening the l/w trough and \npushing a cold front across our area late Tuesday or Tuesday night. \nAs the front approaches, low level S to SW prefrontal winds will \nadvect a warmer, more moist and unstable airmass into our area. \nMeanwhile, with the approach of the s/w, look for mid and upper \nwinds to increase from the NW as the day progresses.  Pre-existing \nprefrontal showers/tstms from the prior day will cross the mountains \nTue morning, with new cells forming downstream across our area along \nthe Piedmont trough and outflow boundary. So look for the chance for \nshower/tstm activity to increase across central NC as the afternoon \nprogresses. The combination of increasing instability and deep layer \nshear will offer the opportunity for any of the taller longer \nlasting tstm clusters to produce localized damaging winds gusts. \n\nGiven the prefrontal warming airmass, high temps on Tuesday should \nbe the warmest of the week with readings climbing into the low-mid \n90s. The showers/tstms, then cold front, are all expected to exit \ncentral NC to our east by 06Z Wednesday.",
